1:["$","$Ld",null,{"item":{"id":"protect-the-result","number":"10","title":"Give the evidence a main route.","mode":"Mandatory detour","scale":"talk","dek":"Reach the specification tests before unpacking the full likelihood.","before":[],"after":[],"diagnosis":"Between the early preview and the detailed tests, the source takes readers through the full likelihood before listing the objects it identifies. Put that map first, then offer the derivation as a branch that returns to the evidence.","changes":["Preserve the early findings preview.","Explain the restrictions and measured objects before the full likelihood.","Keep a core route to detailed evidence, with a clearly marked method branch."],"preserved":"All three restrictions, their maintained assumptions, and the source’s empirical distinctions. The complete table retains the aggregate exception alongside the six industries.","deferred":"The full likelihood and auxiliary estimators move to an optional branch. They remain available from the stage that introduces the estimated objects.","practice":"Build a core of roughly twenty distinct displays, with optional branches. Count animation builds as one display. Rehearse both routes aloud.","principle":"Set deadlines and adjust as you go","sourceType":"public","context":"The deck previews findings on PDF p. 4 and already uses return-linked reserve tables. This proposal extends that structure to the earlier likelihood section. It concerns visible order, not elapsed presentation time.","beforeCaption":"Selected middle slides, in source order; intervening pages omitted.","afterCaption":"","values":["P-values are transcribed from PDF p. 24; values printed as 0 are rounded, not exact zero.","The B/C test adds homogeneous workers and is interpreted by the paper as evidence about directed search.","The rebuilt table keeps all six industries and the aggregate. The log-likelihood levels and LR statistics remain in the linked source."],"outline":{"title":"Give the evidence a main route","description":"A 26-page research deck. Keep the early findings preview, then make the detailed tests reachable through a six-stage core. Put the full likelihood on an optional branch.","before":[{"label":"Source section","title":"Question and preview","content":"Test submarkets, directed search, and efficient surplus sharing. Across six industries, submarkets and direction are not rejected; efficiency is rejected in three.","pages":"2–4","role":"setup","shortTitle":"Question and preview","id":"search-question"},{"label":"Source section","title":"Model and restrictions","content":"Submarket inflows equal outflows. Workers choose submarkets; higher equilibrium wages accompany lower job-finding rates. Efficient wage posting equates the matching elasticity and wage-splitting parameter.","pages":"5–8","role":"setup","shortTitle":"Model and restrictions","id":"search-model","takeaway":"Homogeneous, risk-neutral workers."},{"label":"Source section","title":"Data","content":"CPS: hourly wages, unemployment durations, unemployment rate. JOLTS: vacancies. IRS corporate returns: labor share.","pages":"9","role":"setup","shortTitle":"Data","id":"search-data"},{"label":"Source section","title":"Estimation machinery","content":"Likelihood contributions, estimation and parameter identification.","pages":"10–14","role":"setup","shortTitle":"Estimation machinery","panels":[{"id":"search-likelihood","kind":"image","title":"Complete Likelihood Function","image":"/originals/competitive-search-2009-p11.png","sourcePage":"11","alt":"Complete Likelihood Function"},{"id":"search-likelihood-summary","kind":"text","title":"Likelihood contributions","provenance":"Schematic reconstruction","sourcePage":"10–13","lines":["Employed workers contribute observed wages; unemployed workers contribute unemployment durations. Employment status determines the contribution.","Parameters determine the wage and job-arrival distributions. Auxiliary estimators use vacancies and labor share."]},{"id":"search-identification","kind":"text","title":"Identification and Estimation","provenance":"Schematic reconstruction","sourcePage":"14","lines":["Wage levels and their probabilities; wage measurement error.","Job-arrival rates and their probabilities.","Matching elasticity, wage-splitting parameter, and job destruction rate."]}]},{"label":"Source section","title":"Specification tests","content":"The A/B/C/D restrictions formalize tests of submarkets, direction, and efficiency.","pages":"15","role":"setup","shortTitle":"Specification tests","panels":[{"kind":"claim","title":"Tests of Competitive Search","label":"WHAT THE EVIDENCE COMPARES","items":[{"title":"A → B","text":"Submarket flows balance: job-finding and separation flows agree."},{"title":"B → C","text":"Directed search links higher wages to lower job-finding rates."},{"title":"C → D","text":"Efficiency equates matching elasticity and the wage-splitting parameter: \\(\\eta=\\beta\\)."}],"note":"A: unrestricted · B: balanced flows · C: homogeneous workers · D: \\(\\eta=\\beta\\)","id":"search-restrictions","provenance":"Source content · schematic","sourcePage":"15"}]},{"label":"Source section","title":"Examples, fit, and test evidence","content":"Descriptive data, fitted distributions, and specification-test results for the industries.","pages":"16–19","role":"setup","shortTitle":"Examples, fit, and evidence","panels":[{"id":"search-fit","kind":"text","title":"Observed and fitted distributions","provenance":"Schematic reconstruction","items":[{"title":"Construction","text":"Observed wages · Fitted wages · Arrival rates"},{"title":"Education & health","text":"Observed wages · Fitted wages · Arrival rates"}],"sourcePage":"17–18"},{"id":"search-selected-tests","kind":"image","title":"Industry test results","image":"/originals/competitive-search-2009-p19.png","sourcePage":"19","alt":"Industry test results"}]},{"label":"Source section","title":"Conclusion and supporting results","content":"Conclusions, additional fitted distributions, full test table and extensions.","pages":"20; reserve 21–26","role":"setup","shortTitle":"Conclusion and supporting results","panels":[{"id":"search-conclusion","kind":"text","title":"Conclusion","provenance":"Schematic reconstruction","sourcePage":"20","lines":["Submarkets and direction are not rejected within the six industries. Efficiency is rejected in three."]},{"id":"search-full-tests","kind":"image","title":"Complete Specification Tests","image":"/originals/competitive-search-2009-p24.png","sourcePage":"24","alt":"Complete Specification Tests"}]}],"after":[{"shortTitle":"The empirical finding","title":"The restrictions receive different empirical support.","content":"Across six industries, submarkets and direction are not rejected. Efficiency is rejected in three. The aggregate rejects both direction and efficiency.","pages":"2–4; 19,24","label":"","role":"setup","revises":["search-question","search-full-tests"]},{"shortTitle":"Three economic restrictions","title":"Three restrictions connect wages, waiting, and surplus sharing.","content":"Submarkets balance inflows and outflows. Directed search links higher wages to lower job-finding rates. Efficiency equates matching elasticity with the wage-splitting parameter.","pages":"5–8,15","panels":[{"kind":"claim","title":"Each specification adds a restriction to test.","label":"WHAT THE EVIDENCE COMPARES","items":[{"title":"A → B","text":"Submarket flows balance: job-finding and separation flows agree."},{"title":"B → C","text":"Directed search links higher wages to lower job-finding rates."},{"title":"C → D","text":"Efficiency equates matching elasticity and the wage-splitting parameter: \\(\\eta=\\beta\\)."}],"note":"A: unrestricted · B: balanced flows · C: homogeneous workers · D: \\(\\eta=\\beta\\)","revises":["search-restrictions","search-model"]}],"label":"","role":"setup"},{"shortTitle":"Data and identification","title":"Worker histories and market totals identify the test objects.","content":"CPS supplies wages, unemployment durations and unemployment. JOLTS supplies vacancies; IRS returns supply labor share.","pages":"9,14,16","panels":[{"kind":"flow","title":"Wages and waiting times identify the test objects.","label":"COMPETITIVE SEARCH","items":[{"title":"Observe","text":"Wages, unemployment durations, vacancies, labor share"},{"title":"Estimate","text":"Wage and arrival-rate distributions; matching and sharing parameters"},{"title":"Test","text":"Submarkets, search direction, and efficient surplus sharing"}],"note":"CPS: wages and unemployment · JOLTS: vacancies · IRS: labor share","revises":["search-data","search-identification","search-restrictions"]}],"label":"","role":"setup"},{"shortTitle":"Specification tests","title":"Direction is rejected in the aggregate but not within industries.","content":"Likelihood-ratio p-values for all six industries and the aggregate.","pages":"19,24","panels":[{"kind":"search-tests","title":"Direction is rejected in the aggregate but not within industries.","label":"COMPETITIVE SEARCH","note":"Likelihood-ratio p-values. 0 denotes a rounded value. ¹Homogeneous-worker specifications.","alt":"P-values by row (submarkets, direction with homogeneous workers, efficiency with homogeneous workers): Aggregate 1,0,0; Construction 1,.86,.56; Manufacturing 1,.90,0; Trade/transport/utilities 1,.13,.83; Professional/business 1,.92,0; Education/health 1,.54,0; Leisure/hospitality 1,.85,.28.","revises":["search-full-tests"]}],"label":"","role":"setup"},{"shortTitle":"Distributional fit","title":"Observed and fitted wage distributions can be compared.","content":"Construction and education/health: observed wages, fitted wage distributions, and estimated arrival-rate distributions.","pages":"17–18; 21–23","schema":[{"title":"Construction","text":"Observed wages · Fitted wages · Arrival rates"},{"title":"Education & health","text":"Observed wages · Fitted wages · Arrival rates"}],"label":"","role":"setup","revises":["search-fit"]},{"shortTitle":"Scope of the finding","title":"Efficiency is rejected in three of six industries.","content":"Manufacturing; professional and business services; education and health. The other industry tests fail to reject efficiency.","pages":"20; 24–26","takeaway":"The aggregate also rejects efficiency.","label":"","role":"setup","revises":["search-full-tests","search-conclusion"]},{"shortTitle":"Technical reserve: estimation","title":"The likelihood connects wages and durations to arrival rates.","content":"Separate likelihood contributions for employed and unemployed workers combine observed wages, unemployment durations, and employment status. Parameters determine the wage and arrival-rate distributions.","pages":"10–13","role":"reserve","label":"","revises":["search-likelihood","search-likelihood-summary","search-identification"]}],"change":"Keep the early findings preview. Place the detailed evidence after the essential restriction-and-data map, with the full likelihood on a labeled optional branch.","overviewTitle":"Which competitive-search restrictions survive the data?"},"source":{"authors":"Bryan Engelhardt, Peter Rupert","title":"Competitive Search: A Test of Direction and Efficiency","date":"20 November 2009","url":"https://www.minneapolisfed.org/economic-research/conferences/~/media/files/research/events/2009_11_19/slides/Presenter8-slides-MNFed.pdf","pages":"selected slides from 2–26"},"topic":"Labor markets","point":"Both outlines contain the same restrictions, data, likelihood, and tests. The revision brings the complete evidence onto the main route and moves the likelihood to the technical reserve."}}]
